# deploy/docker/.env.example — copy to .env beside compose.yaml and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && $EDITOR .env
#
# .env is git-ignored and holds this node's secrets. It is the only file that
# differs between nodes; everything else comes from the image. Nothing here is
# ever committed — if a token ends up in a commit, rotate it rather than
# rewriting history.

# --- Identity -----------------------------------------------------------
# The node's name, used as its Tailscale hostname and stamped into its logs.
# Give each node a name you would recognise in a list at 3am.
NODE_NAME=

# active | standby. An active node runs cycles and opens pull requests; a
# standby keeps its schedule but stands each cycle down. Any number of nodes
# may be active at once — per-item claims keep them off each other's work, so
# the role elects no leader; it just says whether this node spends (see
# AGENT_OPS_ROLE in the specs). Anything that is not exactly "active" means
# standby, including a typo.
ROLE=standby

# --- Which services this node runs ---------------------------------------
# tailnet | local, plus auto-update. See the header of compose.yaml.
# Use "local,auto-update" on a machine with no tailnet or no authkey.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=tailnet,auto-update

# --- GitHub --------------------------------------------------------------
# A token with the scopes the cycles need: read and write on the target
# repositories (contents, pull requests, issues), read on their alerts, and
# read/write on the private state repository named by `state_repo` in
# config.json — every node publishes its own state branch there and reads its
# peers' branches to stay warm.
# Prefer a fine-grained token, scoped to the Poetic-Poems repositories, with an
# expiry — and a separate one per node, so a single node can be revoked.
GH_TOKEN=

# The identity the agents' commits carry. Both default inside the image; set
# them here only to override.
#GIT_USER_NAME=
#GIT_USER_EMAIL=

# --- Tailscale (tailnet profile) -----------------------------------------
# A pre-authentication key from the tailnet's admin console. Use a reusable,
# ephemeral-off, pre-approved key if you are provisioning several nodes; it is
# consumed once at first start and the node's identity then lives in the
# tailscale-state volume.
TS_AUTHKEY=

# --- Optional ------------------------------------------------------------
# The image to run. Override to test a locally built image (AGENT_OPS_IMAGE=agent-ops)
# or to pin a node to a known-good build by digest or SHA tag.
#AGENT_OPS_IMAGE=ghcr.io/poetic-poems/agent-ops:latest

# The container's timezone. Affects the cron schedule, so a node in another
# region still runs its cycles on the hours the operation expects.
#TZ=Pacific/Auckland

# The minute of each hour this node's implementation cycle fires (0-59,
# minus whatever config.json's schedule.excluded_minutes rules out — ours
# keeps 0 excluded because poetic's hourly sync workflow owns the top of
# the hour). Leave unset for the default: a stable hash of NODE_NAME spread
# over the allowed minutes, so several active nodes never all fire together
# on the one shared Claude account. The weekly review runs
# schedule.review_offset_minutes past CYCLE_MINUTE, mod 60, at
# schedule.review_hour. Set this only when you want a minute you can
# remember.
#CYCLE_MINUTE=

# The local profile's port on the host's loopback (the container always serves
# on 8787; this moves only the host side of the mapping). Set it when something
# else on this machine already holds 8787 — a second node's stack, say.
#DASHBOARD_PORT=8787

# The scheduler's network MTU. Set this to the host's egress MTU whenever that
# is below 1500 — behind WireGuard or Tailscale, on some cloud instances, and
# in WSL. Symptom of getting it wrong: DNS and plain HTTP work inside the
# container while every TLS connection hangs and resets, so `gh`, `claude` and
# `git clone` all fail as though the token were bad. Find it with:
#   ip route get 1.1.1.1        # names the egress interface
#   ip link show <interface>    # gives its mtu
#DOCKER_MTU=1500

# How often watchtower looks for a new image, in seconds. Five minutes is
# deliberately short: a roll that arrives while a cycle is running is deferred
# by the pre-update hook, and the poll interval is how long that deferral
# costs, so frequent polling is what makes waiting cheap.
#WATCHTOWER_POLL_INTERVAL=300

# A cron expression to roll on instead of polling — six fields, seconds first,
# in the container's TZ. Optional belt and braces to the pre-update hook: it
# puts the roll somewhere this node's cycles are not, rather than relying on
# the hook to notice one. Most nodes leave it unset.
#
# Set it and you must clear the poll interval in the same breath — watchtower
# refuses both at once ("Only schedule or interval can be defined, not both")
# and exits, which leaves this node silently not updating. So both lines,
# together, and the second one empty:
#
#   WATCHTOWER_SCHEDULE=0 40 * * * *
#   WATCHTOWER_POLL_INTERVAL=
#
# Pick a minute away from this node's own cycle minute — `docker compose logs
# scheduler | grep render-crontab` names it — and away from 03:00, when the
# weekly review runs.
#WATCHTOWER_SCHEDULE=

# The Docker Engine API version watchtower's (old) client advertises. The
# default 1.40 is accepted by every daemon this stack runs on; set this only if
# a particular host's daemon needs a different minimum. See compose.yaml.
#DOCKER_API_VERSION=1.40
